<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajiv Gandhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajiv Gandhi was prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1984 to 1989, succeeding his mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a>, after her assassination and leading the Congress to a record election victory. He promoted telecommunications and computing and began cautious economic liberalisation, but his government was damaged by the Bofors arms scandal and the costly intervention of the Indian Peace Keeping Force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. He was assassinated by a Tamil Tiger suicide bomber at Sriperumbudur in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajiv-gandhi.jpg" alt="Rajiv Gandhi" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bart Molendijk / Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajiv_Gandhi_(1987).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rajiv Gandhi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> on 20 August 1944, the son of Indira and Feroze Gandhi, and belonged to the Nehru-Gandhi family, which was associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>; he was not related to Mahatma Gandhi. He attended The Doon School and then the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1966 and became a pilot for the state-owned Indian Airlines. Although his mother was prime minister for much of the 1960s and 1970s, he remained apolitical.</p><p>After his younger brother Sanjay died in a plane crash on 23 June 1980, he entered politics at his mother's urging, addressing a national farmers' rally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> on 16 February 1981. He won Sanjay's seat of Amethi that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 31 October 1984 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a> was assassinated by two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> bodyguards, Satwant Singh and Beant Singh, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-blue-star">Operation Blue Star</a>, the military action against Sikh separatists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>. Pressed by Zail Singh and others, Rajiv Gandhi became prime minister that day, at 40 the youngest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s history. Mobs rioted against Sikhs in the following days, and sources estimate the Sikh dead at between 8,000 and 17,000. In December Congress won 414 of 541 seats in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a>, the largest majority to that date.</p><p>His first measure, in January 1985, was an anti-defection law. He signed the Rajiv-Longowal Accord with the Akali leader H. S. Longowal in an effort to settle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> problem. He appointed Sam Pitroda as adviser on public information infrastructure, and the public telecom companies MTNL and VSNL were developed. He also tried to liberalise the economy and trade policy. His government's handling of the Shah Bano case, in which Parliament in 1986 overrode a Supreme Court judgement, drew criticism, and in 1987 he sent peacekeeping troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, leading to open conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-tigers-of-tamil-eelam">Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam</a>.</p><p>The finance minister V. P. Singh, moved to defence, uncovered what became known as the Bofors scandal, concerning alleged payoffs by the Swedish arms company Bofors. Later reporting implicated Gandhi personally, though in 2004 he was posthumously cleared. Congress lost the 1989 election.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gandhi remained Congress president and was campaigning for the 1991 elections when he was assassinated at Sriperumbudur on 21 May 1991. A woman, identified as Kalaivani Rajaratnam of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-tigers-of-tamil-eelam">Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam</a>, bent to touch his feet and detonated explosives under her dress, killing him and at least 14 others. The Supreme Court judgement held that the LTTE chief Prabhakaran acted from personal animosity arising from the sending of the Indian Peace Keeping Force to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. In 1991 the Indian government posthumously awarded him the Bharat Ratna.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rajiv Gandhi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rajiv Gandhi</b> was assassinated in 1991 in the same year that a balance-of-payments crisis brought India close to default.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Sriperumbudur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.08,72.88&amp;year=1974" aria-label="Mumbai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6521 1457 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rajiv Gandhi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6971 1727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6971 1727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.08,72.88&amp;year=1974">What was here in 1974? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
